+
+# Decision — resolvePointer is strict
+
+## Context
+Other projects import `resolvePointer` and later features build on it, so it must follow RFC 6901 exactly.
+
+## Decision
+- A pointer is valid only if empty or starting with `/`; `~` must be followed by `0` or `1`.
+- Escapes decode `~1` first, then `~0`.
+- Array indices must be `0` or digits without leading zero or sign; anything else throws.
+- `-` (past-the-end) never resolves, since it refers to no value; out-of-range indices throw.
+- Object members are looked up as own properties only, so `/__proto__` or `/constructor` throw.
+- Failures throw a plain `Error`: "Invalid JSON Pointer" for syntax, "does not resolve" otherwise.
+
+## Consequences
+Callers get no silent `undefined`. Later features (e.g. writing at `-`) need their own handling of `-`
+and cannot rely on `resolvePointer` for it.
